<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Durham University</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1832">1832</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as University of Durham</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Durham University is a collegiate university in Durham, north-east <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, founded by an act of Parliament in 1832 and granted a royal charter in 1837. It was endowed with property of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> and cathedral chapter of Durham, and the bishop's castle became its first college. It is one of the oldest universities in England.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/durham-university.jpg" alt="Durham University" width="320" height="374" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Hogweard · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:University_of_Durham_arms.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Between around 1286 and 1291 the Benedictine monks of Durham established a hall at the University of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oxford">Oxford</a>. In 1381 it received an endowment from Thomas Hatfield, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">Bishop</a> of Durham, and became Durham College, which was surrendered to the Crown in 1545 after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Reformation</a>. Attempts to form a university in the city itself were made under King Henry VIII and under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oliver-cromwell">Oliver Cromwell</a>, who issued letters patent in 1657 for a college. A proposal to let it confer degrees met opposition from Oxford and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambridge">Cambridge</a>, and the scheme was abandoned at the restoration of the monarchy in 1660.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The university came into being through the Durham University Act 1832, passed at the instigation of Archdeacon Charles Thorp with the support of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> of Durham, William van Mildert, and given royal assent by King William IV on 4 July 1832. It opened on 28 October 1833 and received a royal charter on 1 June 1837. Degrees were at first open only to members of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/church-of-england">Church of England</a>. Early accommodation was in the Archdeacon's Inn, now Cosin's Hall, and in 1837 the university was granted the use of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/durham-castle">Durham Castle</a>, formerly the bishop's palace.</p><p>In the early twentieth century the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/newcastle-upon-tyne">Newcastle</a> division, made up of Armstrong College and the Durham University College of Medicine, grew to outnumber the Durham colleges. A reform of 1909 made the university federal, with its seat fixed in Durham. From 1959 new colleges were founded on the Elvet Hill site, beginning with Grey College, named after the second Earl Grey, prime minister when the university was founded.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>Durham is a collegiate university: academic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">departments</a> provide research and teaching, while 17 colleges are responsible for domestic arrangements and welfare. Graduates have long used the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> post-nominal letters Dunelm, from Dunelmensis, meaning of Durham. The estate includes 83 listed buildings, from the 11th-century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/durham-castle">Durham Castle</a> to the 1960s students' union, and the university manages the Durham World Heritage Site with Durham Cathedral. Its library, founded in January 1833 with a gift of 160 volumes from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">Bishop</a> van Mildert, includes Bishop Cosin's Library, founded in 1669.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Durham University” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>This has led to speculation, put forward most recently by Bennett Zon of <b>Durham University</b>, that the Latin hymn was a coded birth ode and rallying cry for Charles Edward Stuart.</blockquote><a href="/songs/o-come-all-ye-faithful">O Come, All Ye Faithful</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>University of Durham</b> was founded in 1832 with part of the bishops' endowment, and the Durham (County Palatine) Act of 1836 transferred the remaining secular powers to the crown.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-palatine-of-durham">County Palatine of Durham</a></li></ul></section>
